<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Only the launcher icon's background stays a literal: it must match the
         HarmonyOS artwork's pure black, independent of the theme.

         Everything else now references Material 3 *colour roles*
         (?attr/colorSurface, ?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant, …) instead of a
         hand-rolled palette. Reasons, in order of weight:

         1. The previous fixed light palette made dark mode broken by
            construction: the theme was DayNight but every colour was pinned to a
            light value, so a dark-mode device got light surfaces under dark
            system bar icons.
         2. The Material 3 documentation archived under docs/ states that the
            colour role system is what guarantees accessible contrast, whereas
            hand-picked hex values do not.
         3. With dynamic colour enabled (see ZcodeRemoteApp), Android 12+ derives
            the whole scheme from the system/wallpaper palette, so on ColorOS the
            app blends with 流体云 instead of fighting it. Older devices fall back
            to the Material 3 baseline scheme, which is light/dark complete.

         Trade-off accepted deliberately: the HarmonyOS build's fixed #F8F8F8
         palette is no longer mirrored. Two shells looking identical was
         nice-to-have; a UI that is correct in dark mode and consistent with the
         platform was the goal for this pass. -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">#FF000000</color>
</resources>
